<%# Budget Form Partial - matches Categories design patterns

    ONE OWNER AND ONE SHAPE (two-ledger spec §3). This form has been three things in three plans: a
    mode-aware form serving a category CAP and a pool rule at once, then a pool-only rule form with
    an optional ENVELOPE HALF for the suggestion panel's accept flow, and now a rule on the
    category that holds the money. The envelope half is gone with the layer — there is no pool to
    mint, no name to choose, no account to fund it from and no category to re-point — so the two
    headings that promised one of those acts ("A new Utilities envelope", "Joining your Utilities
    envelope") are deleted along with the `envelope[...]` fields that carried them. What accepting
    a proposal does besides writing the rule is stamp `funded_since`, which `BudgetProposal` owns
    and the panel's own row says before the click.

    THE OWNER PICKER IS A CATEGORY PICKER, on the paths where nothing named an owner. It offers
    this user's EXPENSE categories — an income category can carry no claim (§2), so
    a rule on one would be a claim on money that category never holds — and
    `BudgetsController#new`/`#create` own the one decision about when it renders. Every path that
    ALREADY names an owner is untouched: the Edit link's rule, and the panel's accept link, which
    carries `budget[category_id]` like any other field.

    A HAND-MADE RULE IS A PER-PERIOD RATE, and `basis` rides hidden saying so. That is the only
    shape this form can build without asking a second question ("$X a period" needs no schedule,
    while §3.1's other three rows all need an interval, an anchor or both), and it is the shape a
    user typing a rule from scratch means: money set aside every period. A DATED bill arrives the
    way it always has — measured, from the suggestion panel, with its date and interval on screen
    and correctable. %>
<% category_mode = @budget.category_mode? %>
<%# WHETHER THIS FORM IS ASKING WHO OWNS THE RULE. The controller's answer, not a re-derivation:
    "an owner is already in context" is a fact about how the request arrived (a prefill, a persisted
    rule) and cannot be read off the record once the picker's own choice has been assigned to it. %>
<% owner_picker = @owner_picker.present? %>
<%# THE SCHEDULE IS ONLY EDITABLE ON THE WAY IN. A rule's shape (basis, interval, anchor, item) is
    measured by the engine and submitted back; changing it later is the edit form's business, and
    an existing rule's shape is not this partial's to re-offer. %>
<% new_rule = @budget.new_record? && (category_mode || owner_picker) %>
<% subtitle = "What a category claims each period" %>
<% subtitle = "Pick what this rule is for, and how much it claims each period" if owner_picker %>
<% subtitle = "What #{@budget.category.name} claims each period" if category_mode && !owner_picker %>
<% cancel_path = budget_page_path %>
<%# "RULE", NOT "BUDGET" (design review M1). The screen this form is reached from calls the thing a
    RULE in every one of its own words — the heading above the list, the "New rule" button, the
    band that says which rules nothing fills — and then the form it opened said "New Budget" in the
    largest type on the page and "Create Budget" on its only button. "Budget" is also the name of
    the whole SCREEN, so the old title read as if it created another one of those. The model class
    keeps its name; the user-facing noun is one word everywhere. %>
<%= page_header(title: @budget.persisted? ? "Edit rule" : "New rule", subtitle: subtitle) %>

<div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto">
  <%= simple_form_for @budget, html: { class: "space-y-6" } do |f| %>
    <%= f.error_notification %>
    <%# ERRORS WITH NOWHERE ELSE TO LAND. simple_form prints an attribute's error under that
        attribute's input, so an error on a field this form does not RENDER produces a 422 whose
        only visible text is "please review the problems below". `:base` was always lifted here for
        that reason — an owner-less rule is a fact about the whole record and has no control at all.

        THE OWNER JOINS IT EXACTLY WHEN THE FORM IS NOT ASKING FOR ONE (fix round 1). On the picker
        path `category_must_be_an_expense` lands under the select the user chose in, which is right;
        on the EDIT path the category is a read-only box and a hidden field, so the same message was
        invisible — and that is the one path the validation exists to close, since `#update` is what
        `BudgetProposal` never sees. Duplicating it would print the sentence twice on the picker
        path, hence the branch rather than an unconditional merge. %>
    <% lifted = f.object.errors[:base] + (owner_picker ? [] : f.object.errors[:category]) %>
    <%= f.error_notification message: lifted.to_sentence if lifted.present? %>

    <%# Main Form Card %>
    <div class="bg-white shadow-sm rounded border border-gray-100 p-6">
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <% if owner_picker %>
          <%# THE OWNER, ASKED — the one branch of this form where the category is a question rather
              than something the request arrived carrying.

              `current_user.categories.expenses`, which is both halves of the scoping: the scope
              answers WHAT KIND of category may own a rule and the association answers WHOSE. A
              foreign id typed into a submit is refused a second time on the way in —
              `BudgetsController#scoped_owner` looks `category_id` up through
              `current_user.categories` and 404s — so this select is the affordance, not the
              boundary.

              THE BLANK IS OFFERED because "I have not chosen yet" is a real state on a form that
              opens with nothing chosen, not because a rule may have no owner:
              `Budget#must_have_a_category` refuses the blank on `:base`, which is where this form
              renders base errors. %>
          <div>
            <%= f.input :category_id,
                as: :select,
                collection: current_user.categories.expenses.order(:name),
                label_method: :name,
                value_method: :id,
                include_blank: "Select a category",
                label: "Category",
                hint: "The category this rule claims for each period. A rule is one of the two things " \
                      "that make a category start holding its own money." %>
          </div>
        <% elsif category_mode %>
          <%# THE FORM DOES NOT OFFER THE CATEGORY, which is not the same as the category being
              unwritable: `budget_params` permits `category_id`, so a tampered submission DOES
              re-parent the rule — within the user's own categories and nowhere else, because
              `scoped_owner` looks it up through `current_user.categories` and a stranger's id
              404s. Both directions are pinned in spec/requests/budgets_spec.rb.

              No picker because the page that lists rules groups them BY category, so moving one
              between categories is a re-parenting that list should own rather than a control
              buried in one rule's form. The field is submitted back so the record keeps the
              owner it arrived with. %>
          <div>
            <label class="form-label">Category</label>
            <div class="form-input flex items-center bg-gray-50">
              <%= @budget.category.name %>
            </div>
            <%= f.hidden_field :category_id %>
          </div>
        <% end %>

        <%# Budget Amount %>
        <div>
          <%= f.input :amount,
              as: :decimal,
              label: "Rule Amount",
              input_html: {
                placeholder: "0.00",
                step: 0.01,
                min: 0
              },
              hint: budget_amount_hint(@budget, schedule_shown: new_rule) %>

          <%# WHAT THE RULE SAYS TODAY, beside what a drift suggestion is proposing it should say.
              The prefilled figure is in THE RULE'S OWN UNIT — SuggestionEngine inverts
              `Budget#steady_ask` before putting it on the wire, precisely so that nothing here
              converts it — and the label above already names that unit, so the two figures on
              screen are directly comparable. Shown only when they differ: on every other edit the
              field IS the current amount and repeating it would be one number printed twice. %>
          <% if @current_amount.present? && @budget.amount.to_d != @current_amount.to_d %>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-500" data-current-amount>
              Currently <%= number_to_currency(@current_amount) %> <%= budget_rule_basis_phrase(@budget) %>.
            </p>
          <% end %>
        </div>

        <% if new_rule %>
          <%# THE SHAPE OF THE RULE, measured by the suggestion engine and submitted back. `basis`
              rides hidden because it is not a decision the user is being asked to make here — it
              is which of the two rule shapes was detected — while the INTERVAL and the ANCHOR are
              both offered, because both are INFERENCES and the panel says so about one of them
              already. Correcting a guess in the form it came from is cheaper than writing a wrong
              rule and editing it afterwards.

              THE ANCHOR USED TO RIDE HIDDEN BESIDE THE BASIS, and Henry's real-use testing
              (ruling of 2026-08-20) is why it no longer does: `SuggestionEngine#next_due_on` walks
              the LAST PAYMENT forward by the interval, so a bill paid late once proposes a rule
              whose every future due date is late with it — and the amount on the same form was
              editable while the date that decides when the money is needed was not.

              THE PAIR IS STILL RENDERED TOGETHER, and that is the shape rule rather than a
              layout choice: §3.1's row 3 is `monthly + interval + anchor`, and a form offering
              the interval without the anchor (or the reverse) could submit row 2 with an N > 1,
              which `shape_must_be_valid` refuses. A rule with NO anchor — every rate, including
              every hand-made one — renders neither, and its interval rides hidden. %>
          <%= f.hidden_field :basis %>

          <% if @budget.anchor_date.present? %>
            <div>
              <%= f.input :anchor_date,
                  as: :date,
                  html5: true,
                  label: "First due",
                  hint: "When this bill next comes round. Read from your entries — change it if the last payment was early or late." %>
            </div>

            <div>
              <%= f.input :interval_months,
                  as: :integer,
                  label: "Comes round every (months)",
                  input_html: { min: 1, step: 1 },
                  hint: "Change this if the bill runs on a different cycle." %>
            </div>
          <% else %>
            <%= f.hidden_field :interval_months %>
          <% end %>

          <% if @budget.item %>
            <%# THE ITEM THIS RULE PAYS, shown and submitted back rather than picked. It is what
                makes the rule payable at all — the claim reads its entries to decide
                whether the bill has been settled this cycle (`ClaimCalculator#due_on`) — and it arrived from the engine,
                which chose it by measuring the item's own history. `item_id` is ownership-scoped
                on the way in (BudgetsController#scoped_owners): a stranger's id would write a
                rule against THEIR spending, so it 404s rather than rendering their item's name
                here. %>
            <div>
              <label class="form-label">Pays</label>
              <div class="form-input flex items-center bg-gray-50">
                <%= @budget.item.name %>
              </div>
              <%= f.hidden_field :item_id %>
            </div>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>

      </div>
    </div>

    <%# Action Buttons %>
    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:justify-end gap-3">
      <%# Back where the rule is listed, which is the Budget page — the same destination
          BudgetsController sends a save to. %>
      <%= link_to "Cancel", cancel_path, class: "btn btn-secondary" %>

      <%= f.submit @budget.persisted? ? "Update rule" : "Create rule",
          class: "btn btn-primary" %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>
